        <!-- ============================ THE BOOK ============================ -->
        <!--
          **The spine is the hinge, and that is the whole rig.** This is the second mechanism
          and the first one was wrong in a way that showed: the book used to be a single node
          whose "opening" was `scaleX` 0.52 -> 1 and `scaleY` 1.9 -> 1, which contains no spine
          anywhere. The consequence was that the shut pose was two parallel dark bars with a
          cream filling and read as an equals sign, and the covers never hinged -- they slid
          apart. A reader called it immediately.

          From this camera the spine runs away along Z, so it is a *point* in our view, and
          opening a book is the front cover plus half the page block **rotating 180 degrees
          about that point** -- an ordinary in-plane rotation. `leaf` is that half. `base` is the
          half that stays. The 2:1 thickness relationship a book has between shut and open then
          falls out of the geometry instead of being faked: shut, the two halves are stacked
          (44 units); open, they are side by side (22).

          Three properties and no opacity anywhere, which is the other thing this bought. The
          old rig needed a front board to fade or collapse, a filler to cover the page taper,
          and a gutter to fade in, and every gradual version of that handoff composited to grey
          mud -- see the history in `docs/streak-flame-rive.md`. A hinge needs none of them:
          `leaf.rotation`, `spine.scaleY`, and `book.x` so the spine ends up centred.

          The cross-section, shut, from the ground up:
              0 ..  -8   the back board, edge-on
             -8 .. -30   its half of the page block
            -30 .. -52   the leaf's half of the block, resting on it
            -52 .. -60   the front board
          and the spine capping all four on the left, from -60 to 0.

          **But the authored values here are the OPEN pose, not the shut one, and that matters
          outside the app.** `Ignite` keys every one of these properties at frame 0, so the shut
          pose is established by the timeline and the authored value is never what the app
          draws. What the authored value *is* is what anything that plays a different timeline
          sees -- and `Idle` keys the flame, the core and the glow and nothing else, because it
          has no business re-stating a pose the ignition already reached. Authored shut, playing
          `Idle` on its own in the Rive Editor therefore showed a living flame standing on a
          **closed book**, which is nonsense, and the same was true of any runtime that named
          `Idle` first.

          The scripts in this directory used to paper over it by patching these attributes in a
          copy of the project before rendering, which meant the preview was right and the file
          was wrong. Authoring the resting pose is the actual fix: it costs nothing, it deletes
          that whole mechanism, and it makes a still render of the artboard -- or a runtime that
          fails to find `Ignite` at all -- show a book lying open with a flame on it, which is
          the only sensible thing for either to show.

          **Twenty-two units of paper per half, up from fifteen, and no fore-edge chamfer.**
          At fifteen the open book was a five-pixel cream sliver on a three-pixel dark line and
          read as a ruler with graduations rather than as a book -- the striations filled the
          whole band because there was no band to speak of. The chamfer went at the same time:
          bevelling both halves' outer top corners opened a wedge between them as they stacked,
          which drew a dark notch in the shut book's fore edge and made the whole thing read as
          a battery icon. A rounded corner does the same job with nothing to misalign.

          **The page block is a constant thickness, which is a concession.** Half a text block
          in an open book does thin toward the gutter, and it was drawn that way -- but a taper
          reflected through the hinge leaves a wedge-shaped *gap* between the two halves when
          they are stacked, and a shut book has no gap in the middle of its pages. The shallow V
          an open book rests in is carried instead by tilting each half three degrees, which is
          cheaper and has no such side effect.
        -->
        <Node x="152" y="248" name="book" id="0:40">

            <!-- The half that travels: the front cover and the pages above the midline. Its
                 own origin is the hinge, so `rotation` is the only thing that moves it.
                 Declared first, therefore frontmost, because it passes over the other half on
                 its way round.

                 Note the *reversed* stacking: going up from the hinge it is pages then board,
                 because the front cover is on top of a shut book. After 180 degrees the board
                 is underneath, which is where a cover laid open belongs -- so the flip sorts
                 the layering out by itself and nothing has to be keyed for it. Both gradients
                 are authored to be read *after* the flip for the same reason. -->
            <Node y="-30" rotation="-3.0892" name="leaf" id="0:42">
                <!-- The page-edge hairlines. **They run ACROSS the block, parallel to the
                     cover -- not up it**, and this was drawn the other way round for several
                     passes. It is the most basic thing the drawing got wrong: the pages are
                     sheets lying flat, stacked through the block's thickness, so looking at
                     the block's edge each sheet is one thin *horizontal* band and the shadows
                     between them are horizontal too. Vertical hairlines correspond to nothing
                     physical at all -- they read as a comb, or as a ruler's graduations, which
                     is how every review described them, without anyone (including the reviews
                     that used those exact words) naming the cause.

                     **Dark, at a fifth or less.** Five wide *pale* bars per side, over a band
                     that itself runs pale-to-tan, meant that near the fore edge the striation
                     was lighter than what it sat on. A paper edge is a shadow between sheets:
                     the same ink the copy is set in, nearly transparent, with the alpha varied
                     so the stack does not read as ruled notepaper.

                     **Generated by `smooth.py`, not hand-fitted**, because the leaf's have to
                     be authored in mirrored local space -- its 180-degree flip runs its y the
                     other way -- so that both halves' layers line up across the gutter once the
                     book is open, and stack into an evenly ruled slab when it is shut. Run the
                     script and paste. -->
                <Shape x="55" y="-3" name="ls1" id="0:830"><Rectangle width="86" height="1.2" name="P" id="0:831"/>
